# Compliance / Response Matrix — {{CLIENT}} {{ENG_ID}}

> The completeness spine of the bid. One row per RFP requirement. Every requirement has a row;
> every **Mandatory** row must reach `met`; a row with no evidence is a `gap` → materials-needed
> or research. The response is done when every row closes. Method: `rfp-analysis.md` §2.

**RFP:** <tender name / ref> · **Extracted:** {{DATE}} · **Source:** `1_received/_md/<rfp>.md`

| Req ID | RFP clause (§) | Requirement | Mandatory? | Response / control | Proof required? | Proof source / status | Owner | Gap type | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R-0xx | §<x> | <verbatim or tight paraphrase> | M / D | <answer or operational control> | yes / no — <why> | A-nnn / BR-nnn / buyer form / not-required | <name> | none / answer / proof / control / clarification | open |

**Status key:** `open` · `met` · `partial (exception noted)` · `gap`
**Mandatory (M) vs Desirable (D):** every M is a pass/fail gate and must be `met` before submission.
A **scored criterion that also carries a minimum pass mark** (e.g. "50 of 100 to pass") stays `D`
in this column — but write "(NN% pass mark applies)" into its Requirement text. Failing one ends
the bid as surely as an M, and a plain `D` hides that.

`Proof required? = no` is valid for a process control only when the reason and control are explicit;
it is not permission to write `—`. `Gap type = none` requires either a resolved proof source or a
documented no-proof control. This separation prevents “we know how we will answer” from being
mistaken for “we already hold the evidence”.

An honest caveat is not proof. If the buyer asks for an outcome, KPI, SLA, credential, signature
or reference and the held source does not contain it, set `Gap type = proof` and keep the status
`partial` or `gap` until that evidence arrives. Writing “we do not claim it” protects accuracy;
it does not make the criterion `met`.
